A.text:
Use `Text.Start` and `Text.End`. Both take the text and a character count.

```m
// first 3 characters
Table.TransformColumns(
    Source,
    {{"Code", each Text.Start(_, 3), type text}}
)

// last 3 characters
Table.TransformColumns(
    Source,
    {{"Code", each Text.End(_, 3), type text}}
)
```

Or as added columns in the UI: **Add Column → Custom Column**, formula `Text.Start([Code], 3)` or `Text.End([Code], 3)`.

Use a **comparer** — `Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase`:

```m
// List.Distinct: comparer is the 2nd argument
List.Distinct({"a", "A", "b"}, Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
// => {"a", "b"}

// Text.Contains: comparer is the optional 3rd argument
Text.Contains("Hello World", "WORLD", Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
// => true
```

Same pattern for `List.Contains`, `List.PositionOf`, `Text.PositionOf`, `Table.Distinct` (per-column) — they all take a comparer. If you'd rather ignore case per a specific culture (e.g. Turkish i), use `Comparer.FromCulture("tr-TR", true)` instead of `Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase`.

B.text:
Both take a **comparer** argument — `Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase`.

```m
List.Distinct({"a", "A", "b"}, Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
// => {"a", "b"}

Text.Contains("Hello World", "world", Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
// => true
```

For culture-aware instead of ordinal, pass `Comparer.FromCulture("en-US", true)` in the same slot (the `true` is *ignore case*).

**`Cube.AddMeasureColumn`** adds the measure; **`Cube.AddAndExpandDimensionColumn`** brings a dimension attribute into the filter context (i.e., "expands" it to the next granularity).

```m
cube = Cube.Transform(
    source,
    {
        { Cube.AddAndExpandDimensionColumn, "Product", {"Category"}, {"Category"} },
        { Cube.AddMeasureColumn, "Sales", "[Measures].[Sales Amount]" }
    }
)
```

Related helpers: `Cube.MeasureProperties`/`Cube.MeasureProperty` for cell properties, and `Cube.CollapseAndRemoveColumns` if you later want to roll the dimension back up.
